WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.475 --> 00:00:12.212
For centuries, these two islands 
in the South Pacific

00:00:12.479 --> 00:00:15.048
were home to Māori people.

00:00:15.048 --> 00:00:17.884
They call it Aotearoa.

00:00:17.884 --> 00:00:19.819
the indigenous Māori population

00:00:19.819 --> 00:00:24.491
was rooted in a sacred relationship 
with the land and surrounding waters.

00:00:24.491 --> 00:00:27.193
They organized society and an 
intricate system of tribes...

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:29.229
and subtribes.

00:00:29.229 --> 00:00:30.797
They flourished.

00:00:30.964 --> 00:00:32.665
But by the end of the 1800s

00:00:32.665 --> 00:00:35.135
roughly 60% of them
were dead.

00:00:35.769 --> 00:00:38.872
Due to new diseases, 
displacement, and war

00:00:38.872 --> 00:00:41.107
all caused by Western colonizers

00:00:41.107 --> 00:00:42.909
who called the islands:

00:00:42.909 --> 00:00:44.878
New Zealand.

00:00:44.878 --> 00:00:46.913
Over the course of several decades

00:00:46.913 --> 00:00:50.650
more than 90% of collectively 
owned Māori land

00:00:50.650 --> 00:00:53.053
had been taken by the British Crown.

00:00:53.053 --> 00:00:55.355
They not only took the land, 
but they took a river.

00:00:55.955 --> 00:00:58.058
That river meant...

00:00:58.291 --> 00:00:59.859
everything to us.

00:00:59.859 --> 00:01:02.729
There were hundreds of my ancestors 
that were taken.

00:01:03.096 --> 00:01:04.164
We lost language.

00:01:04.164 --> 00:01:05.398
We lost land.

00:01:05.899 --> 00:01:07.534
We lost our way of life.

00:01:07.534 --> 00:01:10.336
You kind of wonder at times...

00:01:10.336 --> 00:01:11.137
Who am I?

00:01:12.539 --> 00:01:14.407
What am I?

00:01:14.407 --> 00:01:15.442
But today...

00:01:15.975 --> 00:01:18.311
something groundbreaking is happening here.

00:01:19.379 --> 00:01:22.082
A kind of reparations, or,

00:01:22.082 --> 00:01:24.584
as they refer to them in Aotearoa:

00:01:24.584 --> 00:01:25.685
settlements.

00:01:25.685 --> 00:01:28.021
Land and cash settlements for Māori

00:01:28.021 --> 00:01:31.524
accompanied by historic formal apologies 
from the government.

00:01:31.858 --> 00:01:34.961
"Another first: this law includes an apology."

00:01:35.161 --> 00:01:39.732
Our iwi received $87 million 
in cash from the crown.

00:01:39.933 --> 00:01:42.936
17 land blocks are being returned
with deeds and titles.

00:01:43.203 --> 00:01:46.406
$170 million...

00:01:46.906 --> 00:01:48.241
peanuts.

00:01:48.308 --> 00:01:49.843
It really is peanuts.

00:01:50.410 --> 00:01:52.278
I'm here in Aotearoa

00:01:52.278 --> 00:01:54.681
to take a closer look 
at Māori people's

00:01:54.681 --> 00:01:57.517
long fight for sovereignty over their lands.

00:01:57.817 --> 00:02:01.054
And all the mixed emotions and 
outcomes that come with it.

00:02:01.721 --> 00:02:05.291
I'm also here to explore how 
a similar movement for redress

00:02:05.291 --> 00:02:07.961
could gain more steam in the United States

00:02:07.961 --> 00:02:10.730
for Native Americans and 
Black people descended

00:02:10.730 --> 00:02:12.465
from those enslaved in America.

00:02:13.633 --> 00:02:16.503
Māori people have gotten 
the British crown

00:02:16.503 --> 00:02:18.571
to recognize the harms of their pillaging...

00:02:18.972 --> 00:02:21.841
apologize and pay.

00:02:21.841 --> 00:02:23.710
What can we learn about reparations

00:02:23.710 --> 00:02:24.944
from their story?

00:02:36.422 --> 00:02:38.291
At the end of the 18th century

00:02:38.291 --> 00:02:41.594
about 100,000 Māori lived Aotearoa...

00:02:41.961 --> 00:02:44.764
and were part of various tribes 
still known today

00:02:44.764 --> 00:02:48.201
as iwi, and subtribes,
hapū.

00:02:49.435 --> 00:02:51.604
Though they weren't functionally a nation

00:02:51.604 --> 00:02:53.706
they controlled every corner of the islands.

00:02:54.908 --> 00:02:57.076
But in the first half of the 19th century...

00:02:57.410 --> 00:03:00.313
Europeans began to arrive 
by thousands of ships

00:03:00.313 --> 00:03:01.748
especially people from England.

00:03:02.482 --> 00:03:04.284
Far beyond the small settlement

00:03:04.284 --> 00:03:06.553
of British missionaries already living there.

00:03:07.387 --> 00:03:11.624
Disputes start to occur between 
the new arrivals and Maori

00:03:11.624 --> 00:03:15.662
particularly around use of land.

00:03:15.662 --> 00:03:19.766
The settlers saw the land as property...
something to be owned

00:03:19.766 --> 00:03:21.968
which was in conflict with the Māori view.

00:03:22.936 --> 00:03:25.305
And there were new outside threats too.

00:03:27.574 --> 00:03:32.045
Māori learned that the French and 
other Westerners wanted their lands.

00:03:32.645 --> 00:03:35.615
So they began signing this 
Declaration of Independence

00:03:35.615 --> 00:03:37.050
in 1835.

00:03:37.417 --> 00:03:40.153
Rangatira — or Māori chiefs

00:03:40.153 --> 00:03:43.623
used the document to make a firm statement 
to the rest of the world...

00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:47.160
that authority with an Aotearoa 
rested with them.

00:03:47.961 --> 00:03:51.965
The document declared
"he whenua rangatira"

00:03:51.965 --> 00:03:55.034
which translates to "an independent state".

00:03:55.668 --> 00:03:59.339
With new nationhood, they petitioned 
King William IV

00:03:59.339 --> 00:04:01.341
for support and protection.

00:04:01.341 --> 00:04:05.278
And a few years later, the British government 
and Māori chiefs

00:04:05.278 --> 00:04:07.914
saw the need for another formal document.

00:04:08.781 --> 00:04:10.383
There were opportunities

00:04:10.383 --> 00:04:14.120
to both engage further 
with the British Crown

00:04:14.120 --> 00:04:18.358
as treaty partners in having 
a strategic, political

00:04:18.358 --> 00:04:20.460
but also trading partner.

00:04:21.227 --> 00:04:26.833
In 1840, more than 500 Māori chiefs 
signed this document:

00:04:27.267 --> 00:04:30.436
"Te Tiriti o Waitangi" with
the British Crown.

00:04:30.837 --> 00:04:33.606
Te Tiriti is the foundational document

00:04:33.606 --> 00:04:35.008
at the center of the story.

00:04:37.210 --> 00:04:40.179
Its creation opened up the 
gateway to the unlawful

00:04:40.179 --> 00:04:42.382
British colonization of the islands.

00:04:44.150 --> 00:04:45.885
It's been vigorously debated

00:04:45.885 --> 00:04:48.421
because there are stark differences 
between the Māori

00:04:48.421 --> 00:04:50.690
and English texts of the treaty.

00:04:51.524 --> 00:04:55.862
The Māori text says that the rangatira
have "tino rangatiratanga"

00:04:55.862 --> 00:04:58.331
which translates to "absolute authority"

00:04:58.331 --> 00:05:03.436
over their lands, resources, homes,
and "toanga" or "treasured posessions".

00:05:04.404 --> 00:05:07.040
But the English texts states that the rangatira

00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:09.809
"cede to Her Majesty the Queen of England..."

00:05:10.243 --> 00:05:13.846
"all rights and power of sovereignty."

00:05:14.514 --> 00:05:15.815
Those big differences:

00:05:15.815 --> 00:05:18.785
One vision saying you see ceded sovereignty

00:05:18.785 --> 00:05:20.119
the other one saying

00:05:20.119 --> 00:05:22.021
actually, you get to govern yourselves

00:05:22.021 --> 00:05:23.923
and look after yourselves.

00:05:23.923 --> 00:05:26.559
You know, it's quite fundamental, 
really, in terms of

00:05:26.559 --> 00:05:29.529
how things rolled out across our history.

00:05:29.796 --> 00:05:33.132
Within a Māori worldview, 
within a Māori philosophy...

00:05:33.533 --> 00:05:36.235
it's not actually possible to suddenly decide

00:05:36.235 --> 00:05:38.938
to give up that sovereignty to someone else.

00:05:40.006 --> 00:05:42.141
The British proceeded to assert control

00:05:42.175 --> 00:05:44.610
over the islands after the treaty was signed...

00:05:45.144 --> 00:05:46.713
claiming that under British law

00:05:46.713 --> 00:05:49.082
they had the power to
set up a government.

00:05:49.082 --> 00:05:51.517
They appointed a governer, 
William Hobson

00:05:51.517 --> 00:05:56.322
and formally established Aotearoa
as a colony by the end of 1840.

00:05:56.456 --> 00:06:00.093
There were land sales or 
purported land sales

00:06:00.093 --> 00:06:02.161
where they engaged with

00:06:02.161 --> 00:06:04.731
perhaps one of the hundred owners...

00:06:05.164 --> 00:06:06.099
or, you know

00:06:06.099 --> 00:06:08.601
100 people that had rights to a piece of land

00:06:08.601 --> 00:06:09.936
but engaged with 1 person that

00:06:09.936 --> 00:06:12.872
effectively would sell the entire block.

00:06:13.740 --> 00:06:15.408
Where I'm from in Wairoa

00:06:15.408 --> 00:06:19.145
was a really big significant 
purchase of land

00:06:19.145 --> 00:06:23.116
which was framed as a 
sale and purchase.

00:06:23.116 --> 00:06:26.452
But my ancestors, we're told by 
the Crown Purchase agent

00:06:26.452 --> 00:06:29.856
either you sell us this land 
or we will confiscate it.

00:06:31.524 --> 00:06:34.494
And so it those kinds of coercive practices

00:06:34.494 --> 00:06:37.697
which also resulted in 
significant land loss.

00:06:38.564 --> 00:06:42.301
There was resistance of our people to that happening.

00:06:42.668 --> 00:06:45.872
Conflict over land and resource

00:06:45.872 --> 00:06:48.174
came to a head in 1860.

00:06:48.641 --> 00:06:51.077
In the decades long 
New Zealand wars

00:06:51.077 --> 00:06:53.312
Māori people battled to protect their land.

00:06:53.913 --> 00:06:56.849
But they were severely outnumbered 
by British troops

00:06:56.849 --> 00:06:59.519
who killed more than 
2000 Māori.

00:07:00.453 --> 00:07:04.257
Through the wars, the British confiscated
massive amounts of land. 

00:07:04.257 --> 00:07:06.459
Supported by a new law that said

00:07:06.459 --> 00:07:08.561
the crown could seize the land of anyone

00:07:08.561 --> 00:07:10.730
who had rebelled against the Queen.

00:07:11.297 --> 00:07:14.100
Māori were allowed to defend 
their claims to land in court...

00:07:14.534 --> 00:07:16.736
but mounting a defense usually included

00:07:16.736 --> 00:07:20.139
prohibitively expensive legal fees
and travel costs.

00:07:21.340 --> 00:07:23.042
This land confiscation

00:07:23.042 --> 00:07:26.446
triggered a breakdown of the 
Māori economic base

00:07:26.446 --> 00:07:30.550
and disrupted the communal culture 
of Māori identity.

00:07:31.951 --> 00:07:36.122
Plus, diseases that European colonizers
brought to Aotearoa

00:07:36.122 --> 00:07:38.791
continued to kill Māori 
in large numbers.

00:07:39.692 --> 00:07:42.495
In 1840, shortly after Europeans

00:07:42.495 --> 00:07:44.197
began arriving by the shipload

00:07:44.197 --> 00:07:47.066
80,000 Māori lived on the islands.

00:07:47.066 --> 00:07:50.970
By 1896, only 42,000 remained.

00:07:51.938 --> 00:07:54.774
Meanwhile, the Pākehā population

00:07:54.774 --> 00:07:57.143
those who were white or non-Māori

00:07:57.143 --> 00:07:59.612
ballooned to 701,000 people.

00:08:00.513 --> 00:08:05.051
An estimated 88% of 
Māori lands were taken

00:08:06.853 --> 00:08:08.921
It wasn't until decades later

00:08:08.921 --> 00:08:12.158
that Māori activists would force 
the British Crown

00:08:12.158 --> 00:08:15.161
to reckon with this history.

00:08:22.702 --> 00:08:26.205
Even as a child growing up...

00:08:26.506 --> 00:08:31.711
in the first 15 years of our lives,
for me, was around

00:08:31.711 --> 00:08:34.113
how to be a Pākehā.

00:08:34.113 --> 00:08:38.184
It was about how to talk like them...

00:08:39.118 --> 00:08:40.786
dress like them...

00:08:41.053 --> 00:08:42.355
sing like them.

00:08:44.223 --> 00:08:48.427
By the 1950s, New Zealand's government 
had rewritten

00:08:48.427 --> 00:08:50.730
the history of the Crown's colonization

00:08:50.730 --> 00:08:52.899
into a rosy story.

00:08:53.699 --> 00:08:58.371
One newspaper from the early 1900s 
quoted a politician who said

00:08:58.371 --> 00:09:01.507
"The Māoris had always been loyal
to the British Empire."

00:09:01.874 --> 00:09:05.044
"New Zealand was a country that 
had never been conquered....

00:09:05.311 --> 00:09:06.279
or annexed."

00:09:06.279 --> 00:09:08.347
"It was a mutual agreement."

00:09:08.781 --> 00:09:11.517
There has been a real perpetuation 
of the myth

00:09:11.517 --> 00:09:13.886
that, you know, New Zealand 
has great race relations.

00:09:14.320 --> 00:09:18.224
And often the Treaty of Waitangi 
is held up

00:09:18.224 --> 00:09:21.994
as the example of that 
is to say, look

00:09:21.994 --> 00:09:24.931
we negotiated this sovereignty by consent.

00:09:26.265 --> 00:09:28.768
But in practice the oppression 
of the Māori

00:09:28.801 --> 00:09:31.170
had continued for more than 100 years

00:09:31.170 --> 00:09:34.140
after the Treaty of Waitangi had been signed.

00:09:34.740 --> 00:09:37.710
"For the first time in history, 
a reigning British monarch

00:09:37.710 --> 00:09:39.912
visits her Māori subjects."

00:09:40.313 --> 00:09:42.682
Māori military men who 
fought for the crown

00:09:42.682 --> 00:09:45.751
in both World Wars were not given 
the same benefits

00:09:45.751 --> 00:09:48.421
as Pākehā after their return.

00:09:48.421 --> 00:09:50.022
Disparities became glaringly

00:09:50.022 --> 00:09:52.024
apparent by the mid century.

00:09:52.024 --> 00:09:55.628
Compared to Pākehā, Māori had 
poor health outcomes

00:09:55.628 --> 00:09:57.730
like double the child mortality rate.

00:09:58.064 --> 00:10:01.233
They were over 5 times more likely 
to be incarcerated

00:10:01.233 --> 00:10:03.302
and both Māori men and women

00:10:03.302 --> 00:10:06.272
had lower incomes than their 
Pākehā counterparts.

00:10:07.039 --> 00:10:10.109
And in 1953, the Crown introduced laws

00:10:10.109 --> 00:10:13.646
that further for some Māori 
to sell their land.

00:10:13.646 --> 00:10:16.182
Those laws, along with World War II

00:10:16.182 --> 00:10:16.282
Those laws, along with World War II

00:10:16.282 --> 00:10:18.985
helped incite a mass migration of Māori

00:10:18.985 --> 00:10:21.554
from rural lands to cities.

00:10:21.554 --> 00:10:23.956
Living in Christchurch was 
a whole experience.

00:10:24.423 --> 00:10:26.626
Living in a white town.

00:10:26.626 --> 00:10:28.761
You kind of hold your breath and go

00:10:28.761 --> 00:10:32.732
holy hell, I never experienced racism like that.

00:10:33.132 --> 00:10:36.936
As a young boy, I wasn't too sure 
what the hell was going on

00:10:36.936 --> 00:10:38.838
but I knew there was something wrong.

00:10:42.008 --> 00:10:44.610
I can't help but think about 
the similarities here between

00:10:44.610 --> 00:10:46.979
how Black Americans and Native Americans

00:10:46.979 --> 00:10:49.548
were treated in the United States 
around the same time.

00:10:50.916 --> 00:10:53.285
Many Black Americans and 
Native Americans

00:10:53.285 --> 00:10:55.488
enlisted and fought in World War II...

00:10:56.522 --> 00:10:58.290
but upon their return from the war

00:10:58.290 --> 00:10:59.659
they were shut out of benefits

00:10:59.659 --> 00:11:01.527
that were extended to white American.

00:11:01.527 --> 00:11:05.431
Like home loans and educational funding 
provided by the GI Bill.

00:11:05.931 --> 00:11:08.634
Black Americans were segregated through redlining

00:11:08.634 --> 00:11:10.069
and the benefit didn't extend

00:11:10.069 --> 00:11:12.204
to Native Americans living on tribal lands.

00:11:13.172 --> 00:11:16.876
Over time, the compounding impacts 
of racial discrimination in the US

00:11:16.876 --> 00:11:21.647
showed up the same ways they did 
for Māori populations.

00:11:21.647 --> 00:11:23.616
With a higher infant mortality rates...

00:11:23.983 --> 00:11:28.020
Black incarceration rate that was 
just over 5 times higher

00:11:28.020 --> 00:11:29.889
and much lower networth.

00:11:30.956 --> 00:11:35.661
But by the 1960s, with many Maori 
now gathered in cities

00:11:35.661 --> 00:11:39.331
they were better positioned 
to challenge leadership.

00:11:40.332 --> 00:11:44.370
"In America, black people are treated
very much as colonized people

00:11:44.370 --> 00:11:47.006
because we're used, we're brutalized

00:11:47.006 --> 00:11:50.176
the police in our community
occupy our area

00:11:50.176 --> 00:11:52.912
our community, as a foreign group 
occupies territory.

00:11:54.113 --> 00:11:55.481
The advent of television

00:11:55.481 --> 00:11:58.117
helped Māori tune into the 
Black Power Movement

00:11:58.117 --> 00:12:01.487
and the activism of Native Americans 
in the United States.

00:12:01.987 --> 00:12:04.724
But the people have to stand up and demand
and they have to understand

00:12:04.724 --> 00:12:06.158
that they have a right.

00:12:06.158 --> 00:12:09.328
In America you have quite a few 
movers and shakers...

00:12:10.096 --> 00:12:12.364
that make a difference, and that was 
all through marching.

00:12:13.432 --> 00:12:15.000
Members of the activist group
Ngā Tamatoa

00:12:15.000 --> 00:12:18.404
channeled these lessons into their protests.

00:12:18.871 --> 00:12:20.906
They were part of a movement to revitalize

00:12:20.906 --> 00:12:24.944
the Māori language te reo Māori
which had been activelly surpressed

00:12:24.944 --> 00:12:26.378
in schools for decades.

00:12:27.346 --> 00:12:29.915
And later Ngā Tamatoa helped launch

00:12:29.915 --> 00:12:32.918
one of the biggest protests Aotearoa history:

00:12:33.319 --> 00:12:35.488
the Land March of 1975.

00:12:35.955 --> 00:12:38.390
"It's been joined since early this morning
by young and old."

00:12:38.390 --> 00:12:40.459
"And in spite of rain, which set in 
late in the morning...

00:12:40.693 --> 00:12:42.661
the marcheer's spirits are still high."

00:12:42.661 --> 00:12:43.863
It's a huge project.

00:12:43.863 --> 00:12:46.832
A lot of our people actually 
didn't support us.

00:12:47.166 --> 00:12:50.336
They think we're a little bit naive 
or a little bit nutty.

00:12:51.170 --> 00:12:54.840
The protest action needed someone 
who could rally Māori together.

00:12:54.840 --> 00:12:56.575
Her name was Whina Cooper.

00:12:56.575 --> 00:12:59.645
Whina Cooper was a respected Māori elder

00:12:59.645 --> 00:13:01.847
descended from the Te Rahrawa iwi

00:13:01.847 --> 00:13:04.416
who dedicated her life to Māori land rights

00:13:04.416 --> 00:13:06.685
and to the rights of Māori women.

00:13:06.685 --> 00:13:09.522
She was nearly 80 years old 
at the time of the march.

00:13:09.922 --> 00:13:11.624
We need somebody like that

00:13:11.624 --> 00:13:13.759
that can bridge through the heart and the minds.

00:13:14.059 --> 00:13:15.394
So we got the face.

00:13:15.394 --> 00:13:16.762
We need a slogan.

00:13:16.762 --> 00:13:18.397
And so we came up with the slogan:

00:13:18.397 --> 00:13:21.233
"Not one more acre of Māori land be sold."

00:13:21.967 --> 00:13:22.835
Not one more acre.

00:13:23.202 --> 00:13:26.338
After all these 135 years

00:13:26.338 --> 00:13:29.341
we have only 2 million acres left.

00:13:30.643 --> 00:13:34.446
The march took 29 days and 
grew to include 5000 people.

00:13:34.880 --> 00:13:37.516
They came from all over 
the north and south islands.

00:13:38.484 --> 00:13:40.753
And when they arrived at Parliament

00:13:40.753 --> 00:13:44.323
marchers presented a petition 
signed by 60,000 people

00:13:44.323 --> 00:13:45.724
that laid out their goals.

00:13:46.025 --> 00:13:48.060
They were here to establish new laws

00:13:48.060 --> 00:13:51.096
from Māori land based on their 
own cultural values:

00:13:51.397 --> 00:13:54.033
communal ownership of land within the tribe.

00:13:54.667 --> 00:13:58.270
Protest movements anywhere in the world 
makes a difference.

00:13:58.671 --> 00:14:01.307
If you don't protest, 
nothing will happen.

00:14:01.807 --> 00:14:04.143
And people will think 
everything's all right.

00:14:04.143 --> 00:14:05.010
It's not.

00:14:05.377 --> 00:14:07.213
The Crown, long recluctant 

00:14:07.213 --> 00:14:09.915
to acknowledge the harms 
of its colonization

00:14:09.915 --> 00:14:12.084
was forced to respond.

00:14:12.084 --> 00:14:16.222
They established the Treaty of Waitangi
Act of 1975

00:14:16.222 --> 00:14:20.025
to investigate contemporary breaches 
of the 1840 treaty.

00:14:21.093 --> 00:14:24.496
But it wasn't until after another 
decade of protests

00:14:24.496 --> 00:14:27.132
that the Māori won the right to give 
the investigating tribunal

00:14:27.132 --> 00:14:30.035
retrospective powers...

00:14:30.736 --> 00:14:32.972
the ability to investigate grievances

00:14:32.972 --> 00:14:35.441
going all the way back to 1840.

00:14:35.708 --> 00:14:37.443
While this tribunal could only offer

00:14:37.443 --> 00:14:40.279
recommendations for government action

00:14:40.279 --> 00:14:43.082
it opened the door for a kind of reparations.

00:14:44.483 --> 00:14:45.718
The government decided

00:14:45.718 --> 00:14:49.555
to create a pathway to dozens 
of land and cash settlements

00:14:49.555 --> 00:14:51.090
that are ongoing today.

00:14:51.657 --> 00:14:54.226
I really excited about that

00:14:54.226 --> 00:14:57.229
to be able to do some more 
research for us there

00:14:57.229 --> 00:14:59.131
to share our story.

00:14:59.131 --> 00:15:02.167
For us to be able to have this type of conversation.

00:15:02.401 --> 00:15:05.437
At last we can talk to each other about it.

00:15:05.437 --> 00:15:08.340
At last we can have a voice we can sing about.

00:15:08.807 --> 00:15:10.242
I don't have a problem with that.

00:15:10.242 --> 00:15:12.578
It's the end product where
there seems to be 

00:15:12.578 --> 00:15:14.046
something not quite right.

00:15:14.680 --> 00:15:16.382
The settlement was the right thing to do.

00:15:16.382 --> 00:15:18.851
The value of the settlement, 
I think, was wrong.

00:15:19.551 --> 00:15:21.453
When you think about it now

00:15:21.453 --> 00:15:24.356
it's only a drop in the bucket.

00:15:26.325 --> 00:15:30.696
There are about 138 
government-recognized iwi today.

00:15:30.696 --> 00:15:32.498
And they all have their own specific

00:15:32.498 --> 00:15:34.466
historical grievances with the Crown.

00:15:35.634 --> 00:15:40.139
As of 2022, there have been nearly 
80 settlements passed into law.

00:15:41.473 --> 00:15:43.709
These claims generally work like this.

00:15:44.510 --> 00:15:46.812
Iwi must register a historical claim

00:15:46.812 --> 00:15:50.115
with the Waitangi Tribunal to 
have their grievances heard

00:15:50.115 --> 00:15:52.017
and to proceed in the settlement process.

00:15:52.551 --> 00:15:54.486
If their claims are deemed well-founded

00:15:54.486 --> 00:15:57.690
they can begin the process of 
direct negotiation with the Crown.

00:15:58.457 --> 00:16:01.193
After what can be years of back and forth

00:16:01.193 --> 00:16:03.796
eventually, they write the final 
deed of settlement

00:16:03.796 --> 00:16:06.065
that both parties sign.

00:16:06.065 --> 00:16:08.100
To receive the settlement assets

00:16:08.100 --> 00:16:10.736
iwi must set up a governance entity

00:16:10.736 --> 00:16:14.073
that administers the wins 
on behalf of the community

00:16:14.073 --> 00:16:16.742
often putting the money towards 
business development

00:16:16.742 --> 00:16:19.411
health and education for the entire group...

00:16:19.912 --> 00:16:22.147
not checks to individuals.

00:16:23.115 --> 00:16:25.651
The list of what Māori are seeking is long.

00:16:26.418 --> 00:16:31.256
It includes millions of dollars, 
apologies, the return of land

00:16:31.256 --> 00:16:36.495
waters, fisheries, minerals and the 
restoration of te reo Māori

00:16:36.495 --> 00:16:37.496
the Māori language.

00:16:38.464 --> 00:16:39.932
And no two settlements

00:16:39.932 --> 00:16:42.301
or the experience of negotiating them

00:16:42.301 --> 00:16:43.502
are the same.

00:16:44.303 --> 00:16:47.673
But many people live here, 
Waikato-Tainui

00:16:47.673 --> 00:16:50.576
the first iwi to settle in 1995

00:16:50.576 --> 00:16:53.479
as a model for what settlements could be.

00:16:53.479 --> 00:16:56.582
As the first iwi to settle 
they laid the groundwork

00:16:56.582 --> 00:16:58.517
for all the settlements that followed in 1995.

00:16:59.084 --> 00:17:03.655
In 1995, the Crown recognized 
the unlawful theft

00:17:03.655 --> 00:17:07.993
of more than a million acres of
Waikato lands in the 1860s

00:17:07.993 --> 00:17:11.630
and settled with the iwi for $170 million.

00:17:12.531 --> 00:17:17.503
This was a time in this country 
where settlements were new.

00:17:17.836 --> 00:17:19.605
It was against public opinion.

00:17:20.072 --> 00:17:22.141
It certainly was a courageous move

00:17:22.141 --> 00:17:24.276
on the part of the government at that time.

00:17:24.810 --> 00:17:27.279
Queen Elizabeth signed 
this treaty settlement

00:17:27.279 --> 00:17:30.215
which to this day is one of the 
largest sums of money

00:17:30.249 --> 00:17:32.184
given to an Iwi.

00:17:32.184 --> 00:17:36.588
"This is the first time the Queen 
has ever signed any law in public."

00:17:36.588 --> 00:17:38.924
That was the pinnacle of all apologies.

00:17:39.291 --> 00:17:42.061
Most apologies are given by the Crown, 
by the government.

00:17:42.661 --> 00:17:47.633
In this instance, it was the only apology 
made by Queen Elizabeth II.

00:17:47.633 --> 00:17:49.668
That we were the only tribe

00:17:49.668 --> 00:17:52.171
to get that formal apology

00:17:52.171 --> 00:17:55.374
in a signed letter from her is huge.

00:17:56.475 --> 00:18:01.346
Because it was an admission 
of their guilt

00:18:01.346 --> 00:18:03.549
of their wrongdoing and their injustice.

00:18:04.083 --> 00:18:06.919
The settlement was successful
but also controversial.

00:18:07.419 --> 00:18:10.989
For one, the iwi was criticized 
for signing with the Crown

00:18:10.989 --> 00:18:13.792
just months after dozens of iwi nationwide

00:18:13.792 --> 00:18:17.896
rejected the government's $1 billion 
fiscal cap on the settlements.

00:18:18.597 --> 00:18:22.668
And the settlement only provided 
the return of around 30,000 acres.

00:18:23.402 --> 00:18:25.938
It's an insignificant amount of land.

00:18:26.605 --> 00:18:29.108
When you compare with that which we lost.

00:18:29.641 --> 00:18:32.478
One big win was the Te Rapa base

00:18:32.478 --> 00:18:34.980
which they developed into a shopping district

00:18:34.980 --> 00:18:37.382
a move to bring them economic independence.

00:18:38.250 --> 00:18:43.455
The settlement also included the $15 million 
Waikato-Tainui College

00:18:43.455 --> 00:18:45.624
which has helped them invest in education.

00:18:46.458 --> 00:18:49.561
Anyone who wants to do business 
in our tribal area

00:18:49.561 --> 00:18:51.597
and they'll come talk to us.

00:18:51.597 --> 00:18:53.132
We're undeniable now.

00:18:53.132 --> 00:18:54.766
Things have changed.

00:18:54.766 --> 00:18:58.670
In 2010, Waikato Tainui 
finalized another settlement.

00:18:59.138 --> 00:19:01.974
This time it was for the longest river 
in New Zealand

00:19:01.974 --> 00:19:05.210
from which the iwi takes its name, 
the Waikato River.

00:19:05.944 --> 00:19:08.380
We got an agreement that gave us equal say.

00:19:09.314 --> 00:19:12.951
And anything that happened on the river, 
in the river, around the river.

00:19:14.119 --> 00:19:16.788
So for me, as a negotiator, 
for my iwi people, my tribe...

00:19:17.823 --> 00:19:19.591
was the greatest honor of all.

00:19:19.591 --> 00:19:21.627
The job that we did

00:19:21.660 --> 00:19:23.195
was was minor

00:19:23.195 --> 00:19:26.732
compared to the hundreds of our ancestors
who died in the battles.

00:19:27.466 --> 00:19:29.935
For our land, for who we are.

00:19:30.802 --> 00:19:32.838
For our way of life.

00:19:32.905 --> 00:19:35.641
They paid a huge sacrifice

00:19:35.641 --> 00:19:38.310
for us to sit in those offices

00:19:38.310 --> 00:19:42.414
and try to get a deal with the government 
in order to resolve

00:19:42.414 --> 00:19:44.750
our outstanding claims against the Crown. 

00:19:47.486 --> 00:19:50.789
For other iwi the settlements 
haven't been as large

00:19:50.789 --> 00:19:53.525
and they've taken decades to negotiate.

00:19:54.860 --> 00:19:58.697
I'm here at Ngati Maru's 
official settlement ceremony. 

00:20:02.634 --> 00:20:05.170
Where a representative from the Crown

00:20:05.170 --> 00:20:07.839
is going to deliver an official apology.

00:20:11.910 --> 00:20:15.214
The Crown makes the following apology

00:20:15.214 --> 00:20:16.481
to Ngati Maru.

00:20:17.749 --> 00:20:21.453
The Crown's many breaches of 
Te Tiriti o Waitangi...

00:20:22.421 --> 00:20:23.922
left Ngati Maru...

00:20:23.922 --> 00:20:26.892
feeling like refugees in their own homeland.

00:20:28.093 --> 00:20:31.797
Its confiscation of half of the Ngati Maru...

00:20:32.531 --> 00:20:34.499
was indiscriminate

00:20:34.499 --> 00:20:36.435
and unwarranted.

00:20:36.435 --> 00:20:38.971
The Crown acknowledges that.

00:20:39.438 --> 00:20:41.673
Its imprisoned members of Ngati Maru...

00:20:42.140 --> 00:20:44.309
assaulted the human rights of the people

00:20:44.309 --> 00:20:47.279
residing there and forcibly removed

00:20:47.279 --> 00:20:52.017
many inhabitants, destroyed, 
and desecrated their homes

00:20:52.017 --> 00:20:54.019
and sacred buildings

00:20:54.019 --> 00:20:55.954
stolen heirlooms.

00:20:55.954 --> 00:21:02.594
The Crown expresses profound remorse 
and apologizes.

00:21:03.862 --> 00:21:07.933
Ngati Maru's settlement included 
$30 million in cash

00:21:07.933 --> 00:21:10.202
the return of 17 land titles

00:21:10.202 --> 00:21:13.405
and the right to purchase 36 Crown properties

00:21:13.405 --> 00:21:15.540
including the Te Wera Forest.

00:21:16.608 --> 00:21:19.411
I am humbled to have delivered 
these words to you today.

00:21:20.912 --> 00:21:23.815
I know how many of you will be 
thinking of those loved ones

00:21:23.815 --> 00:21:26.418
who were lost before they had a chance

00:21:26.418 --> 00:21:28.854
to hear the Crown apologize for its wrongdoing. 

00:21:29.821 --> 00:21:32.157
I pay tribute to you.

00:21:38.196 --> 00:21:41.133
We all know once you lose your land, 
you lose your culture, you lose your reo

00:21:41.133 --> 00:21:43.502
or you lose your identity, 
you lose everything.

00:21:44.403 --> 00:21:47.039
So we have been in a process of reclamation...

00:21:47.339 --> 00:21:49.808
and rejuvenation and revitalization.

00:21:51.977 --> 00:21:58.684
Ultimately, many Māori see the settlements 
as an avenue for empowerment and restitution

00:21:58.684 --> 00:22:00.652
but they ignore know the issues.

00:22:00.652 --> 00:22:04.156
Some iwi have expressed that they don't necessarily

00:22:04.156 --> 00:22:06.792
believe the Crown apology to be genuine

00:22:06.792 --> 00:22:10.495
and it's something that the Crown does

00:22:10.495 --> 00:22:13.632
for their own benefit to release themselves of...

00:22:14.733 --> 00:22:16.201
I don't know, perhaps burden

00:22:16.201 --> 00:22:18.704
or guilt over those historical breaches.

00:22:19.104 --> 00:22:23.442
Ultimately, the ministers of the Crown 
get final decisions

00:22:23.442 --> 00:22:26.912
on the level of quantum, 
the level of financial redress...

00:22:27.446 --> 00:22:29.381
you know what land parcels

00:22:29.381 --> 00:22:31.650
might be available to come back.

00:22:32.084 --> 00:22:34.486
What what we're giving away

00:22:34.486 --> 00:22:38.357
in the likes of land, water, 
the air we breathe...

00:22:39.091 --> 00:22:41.026
is billions.

00:22:42.227 --> 00:22:44.262
All stolen property should be returned.

00:22:44.730 --> 00:22:47.132
It's like a car. It's a stolen car.

00:22:47.833 --> 00:22:50.435
Your car was taken with everything in the car.

00:22:50.435 --> 00:22:51.870
Everything taken.

00:22:51.870 --> 00:22:54.439
Then they give you your car back with no motor in it.

00:22:55.574 --> 00:22:56.441
Just the wheel.

00:22:56.441 --> 00:22:58.009
You can't move it.

00:22:58.009 --> 00:23:00.011
You can't drive it.

00:23:00.011 --> 00:23:00.812
Yeah.

00:23:00.812 --> 00:23:02.247
We want the whole car.

00:23:04.483 --> 00:23:09.421
Today, Aotearoa is 27 years into 
their settlement process.

00:23:10.155 --> 00:23:13.925
For some, these settlements have meant 
life changing differences

00:23:13.925 --> 00:23:16.628
in business opportunities and education.

00:23:17.963 --> 00:23:21.299
In many categories, like median household income

00:23:21.299 --> 00:23:23.969
Māori outcomes have improved

00:23:23.969 --> 00:23:27.739
but they still fall behind Pākehā.

00:23:31.443 --> 00:23:33.845
I see settlements as a...

00:23:34.479 --> 00:23:35.914
reparation.

00:23:36.448 --> 00:23:39.584
I think reparations are 
far too nice a word.

00:23:40.685 --> 00:23:42.654
You can't embrace the cruelty...

00:23:43.021 --> 00:23:44.489
the injustice...

00:23:45.056 --> 00:23:46.558
the loss of life.

00:23:46.892 --> 00:23:49.027
This is about righting a wrong.

00:23:50.629 --> 00:23:53.331
We're talking about blood here.

00:23:54.266 --> 00:23:56.301
These kinds of complications are echoed

00:23:56.301 --> 00:23:58.470
in the reparations debate in the United States.

00:23:59.171 --> 00:24:01.206
Some people say that there's 
no amount of money

00:24:01.206 --> 00:24:04.142
that could ever compensate 
for slavery or for land loss.

00:24:04.576 --> 00:24:07.145
And people say that reparations 
would further divide

00:24:07.145 --> 00:24:08.613
the United States.

00:24:08.613 --> 00:24:11.650
But what the Māori process shows us 
is that you have to start

00:24:11.650 --> 00:24:12.951
with acknowledging the past.

00:24:13.885 --> 00:24:16.955
"An apology for slavery and 
the Jim Crowe laws...

00:24:18.023 --> 00:24:20.125
which for a century after emancipation..."

00:24:20.492 --> 00:24:24.129
The Senate apologized for slavery 
and Jim Crow in 2009.

00:24:24.696 --> 00:24:28.400
"Today, the Senate will unanimously 
makes that apology."

00:24:28.500 --> 00:24:30.902
But made no mention of reparations.

00:24:31.770 --> 00:24:34.539
And while the US apologized 
to Native Americans 

00:24:34.539 --> 00:24:36.641
for acts of violence and neglect...

00:24:37.642 --> 00:24:39.644
the statement was largely buried

00:24:39.644 --> 00:24:42.247
in the back of a 2010 defense bill. 

00:24:43.748 --> 00:24:47.652
Even though Aotearoa can't be an 
exact model for the United States...

00:24:48.186 --> 00:24:51.389
Māori have a direct message 
for people in the US.

00:24:52.290 --> 00:24:54.993
The fight for reparations is worth it.

00:24:55.660 --> 00:24:58.196
I know it sounds like 
it's never going to happen.

00:24:58.196 --> 00:25:00.732
But truly, there comes a point

00:25:00.732 --> 00:25:02.367
where they have to start to listen.

00:25:03.335 --> 00:25:04.870
The struggle might be long

00:25:04.870 --> 00:25:06.571
and full of uncertainty.

00:25:07.172 --> 00:25:10.809
But if the US continues to push 
the reparations debate forward

00:25:10.809 --> 00:25:13.578
people here could gain 
land, money...

00:25:14.012 --> 00:25:17.415
better policies and new pathways to healing.

00:25:18.583 --> 00:25:20.085
Just like the Māori.

